<!--
    decbench: `returndup` splits the shared epilogue of a source function that is
    ONE short-circuit boolean return, and the split permanently blocks the fold.
      Case: O0-iproute2-ip-sci_complete   (GED 21 with the split, 0 without)
      Siblings: O0-cleanflight-cleanflight_DALRCF405-USBD_DataInStage (13 -> 0),
                O0-crazyflie-cf2-DCD_HandleUSBSuspend_ISR             (12 -> 0)

    Source of this testcase (gcc -O0 -fno-pie -fno-stack-protector -c), the
    sci_complete shape reduced to its four operands and no calls, so the object
    is relocation-free:

        int t(int a, int b, int c, int d)
        {
          return a || (b && (c || d));
        }

    At -O0 that compiles to four `cmp/jcc` operand blocks, two constant
    materialisation blocks (`mov $1,%eax` / `mov $0,%eax`) and ONE shared
    `pop %rbp; ret` epilogue, which is the -O0 encoding of every source return in
    the function, not evidence that the source wrote several.

    Root cause (P8).  `Funcdata::returndup_apply` (option `returndup`, DIV-54
    default-ON) gives every predecessor of the shared bare-epilogue RETURN block
    its own private return.  For a guard clause that recovers the source shape,
    but the operand blocks of a short-circuit expression are predecessors of that
    same block, and the shared out-target is exactly what
    `CollapseStructure::rule_block_or` matches on:

        bl -> {orblock, clauseblock}, orblock -> {clauseblock, X}

    Once each operand owns a private return the guards no longer share
    `clauseblock`, `rule_block_or` declines, and `iteboolean` never sees the 0/1
    select diamond either.  `ActionReturnDup` runs in the `returnsplit` group and
    `collapse_conditions` runs later, so the loss is permanent.

    The fix (part of `returndup`) replays `collapse_conditions` read-only on the
    bblocks CFG before splitting (`kuna_orchain::shortcircuit_shared_targets`,
    mirroring ruleBlockOr's own admission test: one in-edge, two out-edges, not a
    switch, not BlockBasic::isComplex, and a shared out-target) and declines the
    function's splits when one of its own candidates is a fold target.  It also
    protects the block joining two single-exit arms that are themselves fold
    targets, because the FIRST returndup invocation of the schedule's fullloop
    still sees the constant-materialisation blocks in between.

    Stage model: P8 "goto-quality" (S8 CFG rewrite before the final block
    structuring), rendered in P9.  Inert with `option returndup off`.

    Without the gate one source expression becomes a five-return guard cascade,
    `return-dupe x3` on the header; the whole chain folds back into one
    short-circuit condition with a single guarded return.
-->
